## Image and fixture contract

Table 1 distinguishes the incoming Go executable from the actual execution file.

| Role | Binding and use |
| --- | --- |
| Original input | The actual executable supplied by the original Go invocation; its hash, package path and compiled tags are checked and the file stays pinned. |
| Execution image | An exclusive ordinary copy at `<parent>\r\bin\api.test.exe`; copied bytes and the pinned readback must equal the original input hash. No compiler runs in whole-suite mode. |
| Working directory | The original selected-source `internal/api` directory. |
| Arguments | All four incoming arguments, in their original order: paniconexit0, test2json verbosity, count 1 and the five-minute timeout. Selectors and extra arguments are refused. |
| Namespace observations | Four original PID/birth/image/handle-count observations bind to the execution image. |
| Tagged attach Product | A separate actual Product with its own expected hash and `test_state_path_env` build tag; its existing admission is preserved. This Product is not the API test executable. |

Source33 API has no canonical three-file Product fixture requirement for its own
test image. Its peer transport test explicitly uses ordinary Go helpers.
The original helper's actor-image check requires `root\bin\api.test.exe`,
so the new copy path satisfies that exact existing check. It does not rewrite
the helper or turn its path comparison into a hash-only alternative.

The new suite receipt is explicitly `api-namespace-full-suite-v2`,
mode `incoming-go-image-copy`. It keeps original `binary`,
`expected_sha256` and `binary_sha256`, and separately records
`execution_binary`, `execution_sha256` and
`execution_binary_unchanged`. Both images are rehashed after original settlement.

The three original API stderr roots are included alongside the three namespace
roots and the strict peer root in this auxiliary's seven-root receipt. They do
not replace the user's 14 migration plus 2 canonical CLI acceptance roots.
The extra three CLI stderr scenarios remain a separate outer acceptance set.

## Original custody

The existing `runStage` uses the selected production
`RunContainedStreamObserved` owner. Its native and IO completeness predicates,
five-second admission interval, five-minute test deadline and ten-second original
cleanup interval are unchanged.

- **Normal closed return:** original native and IO settlement is complete;
  the original sinks are synchronized and closed. Only then are observations,
  both images and raw stream bytes received. The same captured bytes are
  forwarded once. Path pins close when `run` returns. Known nonzero test exits
  and verification errors remain failures.
- **UNKNOWN:** `namespaceStageCustody` retains the same receipt, original
  error, returned joined error, borrowed sinks and original path-pin handles.
  The original helper process stays alive in a sleep-only park. It does not
  issue a new wait, cleanup, replay, PID lookup, capture read, or subsequent
  command. The unresolved receipt is still UNKNOWN, never CLOSED/PASS.
- **Late original activity:** any original worker can still finish on its
  original object. This candidate does not invent a late-closure protocol or
  substitute newly opened handles. With this source API, unresolved custody
  remains retained until separately and legitimately received.

This corrects a specific recovered727d lifetime defect: its global retained
stage was followed by unconditional path-pin closes and `os.Exit`.
The candidate's retaining branch precedes those actions. No new outer Job
enclosure is introduced.

